# `legacy-peer-deps=true` is load-bearing, not a style preference: without
# it, npm 7+'s automatic peer-dependency installation pulls the REAL
# `@nestjs/common` and `@nestjs/core` into `node_modules/@nestjs/` as peer
# dependencies of the aliased `nest-common-snapshot`/`nest-swagger-snapshot`
# devDependencies below (verified — `npm install` without this setting
# recreates `node_modules/@nestjs/common`). That would make `@nestjs/common`
# resolve inside `test/fixtures/unresolved-module/` and
# `unresolved-module-empty/`, which exist specifically to prove
# `@nestjs/common` is UNRESOLVABLE for `EXIT_COULD_NOT_ANALYSE`'s regression
# test. `legacy-peer-deps=true` reverts to npm 6-era behavior (peer
# dependencies are never auto-installed), which keeps `node_modules/@nestjs/`
# absent except for `@nestjs/mapped-types` (a real, non-peer dependency of
# `@nestjs/swagger`, harmless since nothing resolves that bare specifier).
# See `src/core/nest-exceptions.ts`'s module doc for the full rationale.
legacy-peer-deps=true
